<Page title="How we check facts: editorial and sourcing policy" description="The rules every FBA Tactics page follows: primary sources only, a claims register, independent checking, named human review, dated verification, public corrections and funding." kicker="Standards" photo="hub-about" updated="November 8, 2026" lede="If you find a page that breaks one of these rules, tell us and we will fix it in public.">
  <h2>1. Only the issuing source</h2><p>A fact is published only if it comes from the body that issues it: a regulation in the eCFR, a Federal Register notice, a public law, an IRS publication, a state revenue department, or a marketplace's own public fee or policy page. Blogs, software vendors, agencies, forums, news summaries and wikis are not cited even when they are right. If a reader cannot open the source without logging in, we do not rely on it.</p>
  <h2>2. Every fact is registered</h2><p>Each factual statement links to an entry in a claims register that records the claim, the source address, the publisher, the date it was opened, a short excerpt proving it, who drafted it and who checked it. The site's build refuses to publish a page that cites an unchecked entry.</p>
  <h2>3. A second reader checks every fact</h2><p>The checker is never the person or process that drafted the page. The checker reopens each source and confirms numbers, dates, units and conditions; anything that fails is corrected from the source or removed.</p>
  <h2>4. A named person reviews every page</h2><p>Before a page is published, a reviewer for the publisher reads it against its sources. The reviewer's name and the review date appear under the headline; the build refuses to publish a page without them.</p>
  <h2>5. Dates on everything that changes</h2><p>Each page shows when it was last verified, and each source line shows the date it was checked. Dates are not moved without rechecking the content.</p>
  <h2>6. Nothing invented</h2><p>No invented experts, quotes, testimonials, case studies, statistics or claims of having tested products. Worked examples use hypothetical inputs and say so. Charts plot only sourced numbers on honest scales.</p>
  <h2>7. Questions come from real searches</h2><p>FAQ questions are taken from Google's public search suggestions so they reflect what sellers actually ask; answers come from the official sources.</p>
  <h2>8. How pages are made</h2><p>Pages are researched and drafted with the help of AI writing tools working from the primary sources, then checked and reviewed as described above. The publisher is responsible for every page.</p>
  <h2>9. Photos and graphics</h2><p>Photos come from Unsplash under the Unsplash License (never Unsplash+), or from Wikimedia Commons under public-domain or Creative Commons licenses that allow commercial use without share-alike terms. Each is credited and logged with its license and source page. Photos without clear licenses, with recognizable faces, or with brand logos as the subject are not used. Photos illustrate a topic and are captioned as illustrative. Screenshots of seller dashboards are not used.</p>
  <h2>10. Funding and independence</h2><p>The site is intended to be funded by Google AdSense display advertising. Advertisers have no say in content. Ads are labeled and never placed inside tables, calculators, navigation or source lists. There are no affiliate links, sponsored posts or paid placements.</p>
  <h2>11. Corrections</h2><p>Errors are corrected promptly and logged with the date and what changed on the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.</p>
</Page>
